---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "pescience protein powder",
      "pescience select protein",
      "pescience review",
      "pescience flavors"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Best-Tasting Picks",
    link: "/best-tasting-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">PEScience Protein Powder: Select, Reviewed</Fragment>
      PEScience Select Protein is one of the most popular dessert-flavored proteins sold in the US. Here is what the casein and whey formula delivers per scoop, which flavors buyers rate, how the vegan version compares, and what it really costs per 25 g.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-is-pescience">Who PEScience Is</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#formula">The Select Formula</a>
      <a href="#blend">Casein Blend vs. Whey</a>
      <a href="#flavors">Where to Start on Flavor</a>
      <a href="#vegan">Select Vegan Protein</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#where-to-buy">Testing and Where to Buy</a>
      <a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Skip PEScience</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          PEScience Select Protein is a good buy for people who want dessert flavors without giving up much protein. Most flavors list 23 to 24 g of protein per scoop from a milk protein isolate and whey blend, the texture is thicker than a plain whey, and in the big 55-serving tub it costs roughly $1.50 to $1.90 per 25 g. It is not certified for tested sport and it uses sucralose. For everyone else, it is one of the better flavor-first proteins on the market.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>PEScience Select Protein</strong> is the product most people mean when they search for PEScience, and it earns its reputation. Gourmet Vanilla lists 24 g of protein in 120 calories per 31 g scoop, with 2.5 g of carbohydrate and 1.5 g of fat, per the label at the time of writing. Other flavors land at 23 to 24 g. That is a real dose for a dessert-flavored powder, not a token one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The formula is a blend of milk protein isolate, which contains both casein and whey, with whey protein concentrate and leucine peptides. That gives Select a thicker, creamier texture than most whey isolates and a slower digestion profile, which buyers notice as a more filling shake. It also makes Select behave well in recipes such as protein pancakes, puddings and fluff.
          </p>

          <p>
            The value is good if you buy the large tub. At typical prices, the 55-serving size works out to roughly $1.50 to $1.90 per 25 g of protein, while the smaller tubs land closer to $2. That is more than a budget whey, but noticeably less than many flavor-first rivals that deliver fewer grams per scoop.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">One of the best flavor-first proteins, if you do not need certification.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Buy Select Protein for dessert flavors with a near-full protein dose. Skip it if you are drug-tested, avoid sucralose, or simply want the cheapest protein per gram.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The rest of this review explains the formula in detail, picks the flavors to start with, covers Select Vegan, runs the numbers and flags the counterfeit risk with this brand. If flavor is your top priority, compare Select with the rest of our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> picks before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-pescience" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who PEScience Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            PEScience, short for Physique Enhancing Science, is a US sports supplement brand best known for Select Protein and its High Volume pre-workout. The company built its following on flavor, and its protein line now runs to around 14 flavors on its own site, from Gourmet Vanilla and Cake Pop to Chocolate Truffle, Cookies and Cream, Snickerdoodle and Frosted Sugar Cookie.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the core powder, the PEScience protein range includes Select Vegan Protein, a ready-to-drink Select shake, a Cafe Series, Protein4Oats for mixing into oatmeal, a protein pancake and waffle mix, an unflavored multi-purpose protein flour and a mass gainer. A straight whey isolate called ISOLATE was listed as sold out at the time of writing, so Select remains the brand's main product.
          </p>

          <p>
            PEScience sells direct, through Amazon, and through retailers such as Vitamin Shoppe and Vitacost. Its site describes all its protein products as low carb, gluten-free and third-party tested for purity and potency. The brand does not advertise NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status for Select Protein, which matters for tested athletes and which we come back to below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The audience is broad: gym-goers who want a shake that tastes like dessert, dieters who use protein to replace sweet snacks, and home cooks who use the powder in recipes. That positions PEScience directly against brands like Devotion, which we compare in our <a href="/devotion-protein-powder-review/">Devotion protein powder review</a>, and against the dessert flavors of mainstream whey brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The PEScience Picks: Three Select Flavors and the Vegan Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            Select Protein uses one base formula across its flavors, so the picks below are the flavors and formats worth starting with, plus Select Vegan for dairy-free buyers and one clearly labeled alternative from another brand for bakers. Figures come from PEScience labels and retailer listings at the time of writing and vary slightly by flavor, so check the tub you buy. We ranked on label analysis, buyer feedback and approximate cost per 25 g, not our own taste or lab tests.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="formula" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Select Formula, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Gourmet Vanilla ingredient list reads: Select protein blend of milk protein isolate (casein and whey protein), whey protein concentrate 80 percent and leucine peptides, then salt, natural and artificial flavors, guar gum, sucralose and acesulfame potassium. The label states it contains milk and soy. It is a fairly short list for a flavored protein, with no proprietary amounts hiding fillers.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Label item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Select Protein, Gourmet Vanilla (per 31 g scoop)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2.5 g, 1.5 g sugar</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 230 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame potassium</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein figures vary a little by flavor. Gourmet Vanilla and Cake Pop list 24 g per 31 g scoop, Chocolate Truffle lists 23 g per 33 g scoop at 125 calories, and Chocolate Peanut Butter Cup lists 23 g per 32.5 g scoop, per retailer labels at the time of writing. The vanilla label also shows about 280 mg of calcium, a useful bonus from the milk protein isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine peptides deserve a note. They are a disclosed part of the protein blend that raises the leucine content, and PEScience lists roughly 5 g of branched-chain amino acids per scoop. Because they are listed openly in the blend rather than added as cheap free amino acids such as glycine, this is not amino spiking in the usual sense, but it does mean a small share of the protein figure comes from added leucine rather than whole milk protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            PEScience also says Select is very low in lactose, about 50 percent lower than 100 percent whey concentrate products. That is the brand's own claim, and it is plausible given that milk protein isolate is filtered. People with mild lactose sensitivity may tolerate it; anyone with a milk allergy should not use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="blend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Casein Blend vs. Whey: What It Means for You
          </h2>

          <p>
            Milk protein isolate is roughly 80 percent casein and 20 percent whey, the same ratio as in milk. Casein forms a gel in the stomach and releases amino acids more slowly, while whey digests quickly. Select combines the two, so a shake hits a little more slowly and stays with you longer than a pure whey isolate, which many buyers describe as being more filling.
          </p>

          <p>
            For muscle building, the difference is small. Total daily protein matters far more than digestion speed, and a 24 g dose of milk protein provides enough leucine to stimulate muscle protein synthesis in most adults. If you want a fast post-workout shake, whey isolate is marginally quicker, but research does not suggest this makes a meaningful difference to results over time. Our <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">when to take protein powder</a> guide covers timing in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the blend helps is texture and versatility. Casein thickens, so Select makes a creamier shake with less liquid and works well for protein pudding, overnight oats and protein fluff. It also bakes better than pure whey, which tends to dry out. If you want a dedicated slow protein for bedtime, compare Select with the options in our <a href="/best-casein-protein-powder/">best casein protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is that Select is a little heavier in the stomach for some people, and the whey concentrate portion carries slightly more lactose and fat than an isolate. If you want the lightest, fastest shake possible, a straight isolate from our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> rankings is a better fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Where to Start on Flavor
          </h2>

          <p>
            We did not taste-test the range, so this section reflects how PEScience positions each flavor and what buyers consistently report. Flavor is the main reason people choose PEScience, and the brand's reviews on Amazon and retailer sites focus heavily on it. Start with one of the flavors below, then branch out once you know how sweet you like your shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gourmet Vanilla</strong> is the safest first tub because it works in everything: shakes, oats, coffee, pancakes and baking. <strong>Cake Pop</strong> is one of the brand's signature dessert flavors, a sweeter birthday-cake profile with the same 24 g of protein. <strong>Chocolate Truffle</strong> is the richer chocolate option, at 23 g per slightly larger scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            The wider range includes Cookies and Cream, Snickerdoodle, Peanut Butter Cookie, Frosted Chocolate Cupcake, Frosted Sugar Cookie and Chocolate Peanut Butter Cup, among others. Sweetness is the most common buyer complaint across the range, as it is with most sucralose-sweetened powders, so if you find mainstream protein too sweet, mix Select with more liquid or with unsweetened milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            PEScience sells a sample kit and variety packs, which are the cheapest way to try several flavors without buying full tubs. If you are choosing specifically between chocolate or vanilla powders across brands, our <a href="/best-chocolate-protein-powder/">best chocolate protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">best vanilla protein powder</a> guides compare Select with its closest rivals.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vegan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Select Vegan Protein: Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Select Vegan Protein</strong> blends pea protein concentrate and brown rice protein concentrate for about 20 g of protein per scoop, with roughly 100 calories for vanilla and 110 for chocolate, per retailer labels at the time of writing. It is sweetened only with stevia, and it is free of dairy, soy and gluten. Flavors include Chocolate Bliss, Vanilla Indulgence, Peanut Butter Delight, Wild Berry and Cinnamon Delight.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pea and rice combination is the classic plant pairing, because pea is rich in lysine and rice in methionine, so together they give a rounder amino acid profile than either alone. The 20 g dose is a little lower than Select's 24 g, and sodium is relatively high, around 290 to 330 mg per scoop depending on the listing, which is worth noting if you watch salt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyer feedback is more mixed than for the whey-based Select. Some reviewers note a chalky texture and a noticeable stevia aftertaste, which is common across stevia-sweetened plant proteins. PEScience lists it at about $45 for roughly 27 servings, which is about $2 per 25 g of protein, competitive for a flavored plant powder but not cheap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because plant proteins tend to carry more heavy metals than dairy, testing matters more here. One retailer listing describes Select Vegan as third-party tested for heavy metals and pesticides; ask PEScience for results if that is important to you. For a wider comparison, see our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">best plant-based protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

