        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Testing, Fakes and Where to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            PEScience says its protein products are third-party tested for purity and potency, but it does not advertise NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Choice or Informed Sport status for Select Protein at the time of writing. That is fine for most buyers, but it rules Select out for athletes who compete under anti-doping rules and need a product listed in a public certification database.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a counterfeit risk worth knowing about. While researching this review, we found several lookalike websites using PEScience's name on unusual domains and offering its products. A brand this popular is an obvious target, and a fake protein can be anything from underdosed to contaminated. Treat any PEScience deal far below normal pricing on an unfamiliar site as a red flag.
          </p>

          <p>
            The safe channels are PEScience's own site, Amazon listings sold by PEScience or shipped from Amazon's own inventory, and established retailers such as Vitamin Shoppe and Vitacost. On Amazon, check the seller name and avoid third-party sellers offering steep discounts on sealed tubs. When the tub arrives, check the seal, lot number and best-by date.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, buy the right size for your habit. The 55-serving tub is the best value, but it lasts nearly two months at one shake a day. If you are trying a new flavor, start with a smaller tub or the sample kit, then move up once you know it is a flavor you will finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip PEScience
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip PEScience if you are a drug-tested athlete. Without an NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing to check, you cannot verify that the product has been screened for banned substances under a sport program. A certified isolate such as Thorne's is the safer choice; our <a href="/thorne-protein-powder-review/">Thorne protein powder review</a> explains what that certification covers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you avoid artificial sweeteners. Select uses sucralose and acesulfame potassium, and buyers who are sensitive to sweetness often find the dessert flavors strong. Select Vegan uses stevia only, but it is a different product with a lower protein dose and mixed texture reviews.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you want the lightest, fastest-digesting shake. The casein in milk protein isolate makes Select thicker and slower to digest, which many people like but some find heavy. A straight whey isolate is the better pick for a light post-workout drink.
          </p>

          <p>
            And check with a clinician first if you have a milk allergy, kidney disease, are pregnant or take medication that affects protein needs. Select contains milk proteins and lists soy on the label. Nothing on this page is medical advice, and a doctor or registered dietitian is the right person to help with protein decisions in those situations.
          </p>
        </div>

